  "analyzed_vuln": {
    "function": "dissect_softether_host_fqdn",
    "file": "ndpi/src/lib/protocols/softether.c",
    "description": "The SoftEther host/FQDN parser performs a bounded string comparison with an attacker-derived parsed field length: `strncmp(val2.value.ptr.value_str, \"ddns_fqdn\", val2.value_size)` (and similarly uses the serialized `value_siz` for `host_name`). It does not first require that the field have exactly the expected literal length or otherwise cap the comparison to that fixed length. With malformed tuple size/accounting at the end of the packet, this lets `strncmp` walk past the readable packet allocation, producing the one-byte use-after-poison. The vulnerable location is the caller performing the unsafe comparison, not the ASan interceptor; patch by validating the parsed DATA field length against `NDPI_STATICSTRING_LEN(\"ddns_fqdn\")` and comparing only that constant length (and use `val1.value_size`, with an exact expected-length check, for `host_name`).",
    "conditions": "A UDP packet must reach the SoftEther dissector (the initial path accepts payloads at least 99 bytes) and encode a nonzero, superficially valid SoftEther tuple count and tuple types/sizes. A crafted DATA tuple positioned near the end of the UDP payload supplies the `ddns_fqdn` marker/prefix while malformed serialized length/accounting causes the parsed comparison extent to reach beyond the packet buffer. The fuzzer passes the raw PoC as an IP packet to `ndpi_detection_process_packet`; UDP payload extraction leads through `ndpi_search_softether` into `dissect_softether_host_fqdn`, where the attacker-controlled comparison length causes the out-of-bounds read."
  },
